1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,671
Hi!

2
00:00:00,671 --> 00:00:03,530
We're TerraAegis, a two-person team from Vietnam.

3
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,069
And this is HealthPulse

4
00:00:05,069 --> 00:00:08,710
a climate-informed early warning and response planning tool.

5
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:10,163
Here's the problem.

6
00:00:10,163 --> 00:00:12,537
Climate is shifting when and where disease hits

7
00:00:12,537 --> 00:00:18,258
and the ILO estimates 2.4 billion workers face excessive heat at work.

8
00:00:18,258 --> 00:00:22,904
Yet most health teams still react after cases are reported.

9
00:00:22,904 --> 00:00:26,410
By then, the weeks you needed to prepare are gone.

10
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:28,596
HealthPulse turns public climate

11
00:00:28,596 --> 00:00:33,951
disease and population data into forecasts with honest uncertainty ranges

12
00:00:33,951 --> 00:00:38,016
compares demand against resources, and, this is key

13
00:00:38,016 --> 00:00:40,260
leaves the final decision to a person.

14
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:42,341
Let me show you the working app.

15
00:00:42,341 --> 00:00:45,222
First, a hard question: would it have warned us?

16
00:00:45,222 --> 00:00:49,746
We replayed Brazil's record 2023–24 dengue season, week by week

17
00:00:49,746 --> 00:00:52,247
using only the data available at the time.

18
00:00:52,247 --> 00:00:56,911
All 19 epidemic states were warned early, a median of 6 weeks ahead.

19
00:00:56,911 --> 00:01:00,790
To be fair, a simple rule gets 4, so the model adds about 2.

20
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:02,228
Here's Minas Gerais.

21
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:04,348
The blue line is our alert, in mid-December.

22
00:01:04,348 --> 00:01:07,999
The red line is when threshold monitoring would have seen it, late January.

23
00:01:07,999 --> 00:01:12,000
That gap is time to send vector-control teams, and staff up clinics.

24
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,528
Next, the 2026–27 season outlook, with its 80% range.

25
00:01:16,528 --> 00:01:17,431
Then the brief.

26
00:01:17,431 --> 00:01:22,231
Our AI analyst, Claude, writes it live, from exactly these numbers

27
00:01:22,231 --> 00:01:25,119
with a rule-based fallback if it's offline.

28
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:26,671
And it can only propose.

29
00:01:26,671 --> 00:01:31,640
An epidemiology reviewer approves or rejects, and it's logged for audit.

30
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:33,754
Now, Viet Nam, our home.

31
00:01:33,754 --> 00:01:35,318
All 34 new provinces

32
00:01:35,318 --> 00:01:39,640
with NASA weather data and a climate-driven dengue model.

33
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:43,234
You can see which provinces are on alert this month, and why.

34
00:01:43,234 --> 00:01:48,057
Hà Nội is estimated at about 1.7 times its seasonal norm.

35
00:01:48,057 --> 00:01:51,740
And every number is labelled: observed, estimate, or scenario.

36
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:57,345
In the outbreak sandbox, planners seed an outbreak on the real map, press play

37
00:01:57,345 --> 00:01:59,778
and test distancing, vaccination or vector control

38
00:01:59,778 --> 00:02:02,790
then watch hospital load across 16 trials.

39
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:07,534
And for a real emergency, there's Live response, an incident command room.

40
00:02:07,534 --> 00:02:12,508
In this drill, cases are keyed in as they arrive, and clusters appear on the map.

41
00:02:12,508 --> 00:02:16,755
The AI analyst uses tools over the live data to recommend next steps

42
00:02:16,755 --> 00:02:19,625
and the optimiser proposes where resources go.

43
00:02:19,625 --> 00:02:21,070
Again, people approve.

44
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:22,591
So, does it work?

45
00:02:22,591 --> 00:02:26,070
On held-out seasons across 26 Brazilian states

46
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:30,737
83% of actual counts fell inside our predicted range

47
00:02:30,737 --> 00:02:33,340
versus 75% for the baseline.

48
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:38,220
Better, but still short of our 90% target, and we say so.

49
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:43,082
Next, we want to pilot this with a local health or workplace team in Vietnam

50
00:02:43,082 --> 00:02:46,714
then add validated heat exposure inputs for outdoor workers.

51
00:02:46,714 --> 00:02:49,770
No wearables, no personal data, just a browser.

52
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:52,233
HealthPulse: from signals to earlier decisions.

53
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:53,160
Thank you!
